    },
    {
      "title": "Other uses for Cut 'n Sew",
      "slug": "other-uses-for-cut-n-sew",
      "displayOrder": 6,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 781,
        "lessonOrder": 6
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Fast finishing, accurate size for the opening. Use Cut 'n Sew to form the center opening for your Tree Skirt. <br>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"/challenge/14/course/christmas-tree-skirt\" target=\"_blank\"><figure><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/tree_skirt/tree_skirt.png\"><figcaption align=\"center\"><br>Complete Course for knitting your own skirt</figcaption>\r\n</figure></a>\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9548,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Christmas Tree Skirt",
          "slug": "christmas-tree-skirt",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6230,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "A pillow project that is fast, fast, fast! Use the cut 'n sew technique to create a heart shape. Put your serger or sewing machine to work and have a gift or a craft fair item in no time!<br>We knit a Valentine pillow, but consider other shapes ... how about a tooth shape for a Tooth Fairy Pillow?<br>You can knit in any stitch pattern, fairisle,stripes, tuck, slip or intarsia. Long floats aren't a problem and you don't have to worry about shaping and working a stitch pattern at the same time.<br>\r\n\r\n<figure>\r\n\r\n\r\n<a href=\"/path/176/heavenly-hearts\"><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/path/images/101/Heart_pillow_final.jpg\">\r\n<figcaption align=\"center\"><br>Heavenly Hearts Quick Win</figcaption>\r\n</figure>\r\n</a>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9550,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Pillow Shapes",
          "slug": "pillow-shapes",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6231,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Collar with Ribber",
      "slug": "collar-with-ribber",
      "displayOrder": 7,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 787,
        "lessonOrder": 7
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\n.underlineit {\r\ntext-decoration: underline;}\r\n</style>\r\nAdding a collar to a cut 'n sew neckline is similar to a 2 piece neckband with a few extra steps.<br>What you must know:<br>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li> Have a SOLID understanding of your ribbber</li>\r\n<li>Be familar with your ribber and knit a rectangle in <span data-GlossaryId=\"184\" class=\"glossaryhelp underlineit\">Full Needle Rib</span> or the needle arrangement of your choice <span data-GlossaryId=\"321\" class=\"glossaryhelp underlineit\">(Flat Rib</span> is a good choice)</li>\r\n<li> <span data-GlossaryId=\"328\" class=\"glossaryhelp underlineit\">Circular Knitting</span> (tubular)</li>\r\n<li>Be comfortable transferring stitches between the beds</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9578,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "876386142",
              "title": null,
              "legacyComponentId": 9593,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Sandwich Band with FNR Collar",
          "slug": "sandwich-band-with-fnr-collar",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6256,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">Build your skills and confidence with practice</span><br>\r\n\r\nAt first, instead of knitting and creating an entire cut 'n sew neckline, consider working this exercise along the edge of an existing swatch.<br>The yarns don't even have to match!\r\nThere are a lot of steps.  If disaster happens you can easily rip out the sample, or use another edge of the swatch to try again <i class=\"fa fa-smile-o fa-2x\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i><br><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/swatch1.jpg\">\r\n<div class=\"text-center\"> Save time, use the edge of a swatch</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/swatch2.jpg\">\r\n<div class=\"text-center\"> Use a contrasting color to make it easier to see the stitches as you are practicing</div>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9592,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Collar Exercise",
          "slug": "collar-exercise",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 6265,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Oops Lessons",
      "slug": "oops-lessons",
      "displayOrder": 8,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 788,
        "lessonOrder": 8
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\n    hr.style13 {\r\n        height: 10px;\r\n        border: 0;\r\n        box-shadow: 0 10px 10px -10px #8c8b8b inset;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    span.green {\r\n        background: #60C447;\r\n        border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        -moz-border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        -webkit-border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        color: #ffffff;\r\n        display: inline-block;\r\n        font-weight: bold;\r\n        line-height: 1.6em;\r\n        margin-right: 15px;\r\n        text-align: center;\r\n        width: 1.6em;\r\n    }\r\n.yellow_highlight {\r\n\r\nbackground: linear-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,0) 55%,#ffff00 45%,#ffff00);\r\n}\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops1.png\">\r\n\r\n\r\n    Plan your shoulder join. When knitting pullovers, machine knitters can't knit our bands in the round. There needs to be a seam. (Normally on the left shoulder)<br>When forming your band be sure to add AT LEAST 1 extra seaming stitch on each end.<br>\r\n        In this <b>Oops</b> the red is the bind off neckline edge. You can see that there aren't enough stitches to cover that edge and join the band.\r\n        <div class=\"videopopup\" data-videotitle=\"Seaming Bands\" data-vimeoid=\"151857806\">\r\n            <img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"https://i.vimeocdn.com/video/551701425-2e60883bd433a2c6a08cff085005fec06731a2afb6cd82b5168c720877c0f8e0-d_1280x720?r=pad\">\r\n            <div class=\"imagecenter text-center\"><button class=\"btn btn-sm\"><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play fa-2x learn\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></button></div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n    <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/extra_stitches.jpg\">\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops1.png\">\r\n<h3>One-Piece band</h3>\r\n\r\n    <br><span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">\"Do as I say ... not as I did\"<br></span><br>\r\n        This neckline will not lay flat against the body. By grading you will make sure that the inner curve of the band is slightly smaller than the outer curve.<br><a data-GlossaryId=\"617\" class=\"glossaryhelp\">Grade the Band Tension <i class=\"fa fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a>\r\n<br><span class=\"yellow_highlight\">Another tip:</span>  Run a strand of ravel cord inside the band and pull up, steam to get it to lay flat.\r\n    \r\n    <img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/grade.jpg\">\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops1.png\"><br><span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">2 Piece band:</span>\r\n\r\n    \r\n        <h3><span class=\"green\">1</span>Scrap On (yellow)</h3>\r\nOops,<span class=\"yellow_highlight\"> <b>don't forget the ravel cord</b> </span>between the yellow section and the band. It makes it hard to remove the waste yarn when finishing the band ......\r\n        <h3><span class=\"green\">2</span>Knit 1/2 of the band (red) grading the tension</h3>\r\n        <h3><span class=\"green\">3</span>Scrap off (blue)</h3>\r\n\r\n    \r\n     <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops_ravel_cord.jpg\">\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9581,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\n    hr.style13 {\r\n        height: 10px;\r\n        border: 0;\r\n        box-shadow: 0 10px 10px -10px #8c8b8b inset;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    span.green {\r\n        background: #60C447;\r\n        border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        -moz-border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        -webkit-border-radius: 0.8em;\r\n        color: #ffffff;\r\n        display: inline-block;\r\n        font-weight: bold;\r\n        line-height: 1.6em;\r\n        margin-right: 15px;\r\n        text-align: center;\r\n        width: 1.6em;\r\n    }\r\n.yellow_highlight {\r\n\r\nbackground: linear-gradient(rgba(255,255,255,0) 55%,#ffff00 45%,#ffff00);\r\n}\r\n</style>\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops1.png\">\r\n<span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">Cut raw edges:</span>\r\n<h3>Secure the raw edges</h3>\r\n\r\n\r\nThe stitching line is secured with straight and zig zag stitches and the neckline is cut.\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/raw_edges.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\r\n<h3>BUT ... </h3> When the seam allowance is enclosed in the band, the cut edges tend to pop out<br> Serging (or zig zag stitching) to secure the cut floats would prevent this.\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/popped.jpg\">\r\n<br>\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops1.png\">\r\n<span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">Sandwich band:</span>\r\n<h3>Dropped Stitch</h3>\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen I was rehanging the ribber stitches from scrap, I missed one.  I knew there was a problem when I got to the end of the row, but didn't identify which stitch until I removed the waste yarn.<br>Lesson learned: Remove the waste yarn to double check<br>Because this was just a test neckline, I didn't panic, I just put the stitch on a nearby needle (so there wasn't a hole) and fudged at the edge.\r\n<br>Obviously if this was a sweater, I would have fixed this in a better way .. if you look closely in some of the \"finished\" images, you can see the boo-boo. <i class=\"fa fa-smile-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\r\n\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/116/oops_gif.gif\">\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 9587,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "<span class=\"badge\">NEW</span>",
          "slug": "span-class-badge-new-span",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
